@@ -69,6 +69,27 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+To pretty print a JSON string, use :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json`. The output is valid JSON, indented by four spaces by default (set ``indent`` to change this), and highlighted unless ``highlight=False`` is passed::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+You can also log a JSON renderable directly with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`. The renderable is :class:`rich.json.JSON`, which is created with the same ``json``, ``indent``, and ``highlight`` arguments::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.log(JSON('{"name": "rich"}'))
+
+The :func:`rich.print_json` function is also available at package level and prints using the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('{"name": "rich"}')
+
+JSON can also be pretty printed from the command line by running the json module with a path to a file::
+
+    python -m rich.json FILE
+
